00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.530
And then, next steps
on how you can get

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.240
involved with both Airflow
and also Cloud Composer.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.510
So before Composer,
there were a few ways

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.780
to create and schedule
and manage workflows

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.280
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.140
And to be totally honest,
they were not the best at all.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.270
So on one end, you
had very low cost,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.480
very easy, but pretty inflexible
and not so powerful solutions.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.130
Mainly, just putting
something in a crontab,

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.010
scheduling it, and
letting it run.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.630
On the more complicated
end, you had

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:56.160
customers that were developing
really complicated frameworks

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.130
to schedule, orchestrate,
and manage things

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.960
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.950
Really powerful, but also took
a team of engineers to do.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.190
In our opinion, none of
this is an ideal solution.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.606
Because people are focusing on
things that are not really what

00:02:11.606 --> 00:02:12.480
they're trying to do.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.480
They're developing
orchestration engines.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.720
They're developing
description languages.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.720
They're not focusing on
what they set out to do,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.770
which was just
having a workflow run

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.210
and monitoring that
workflow from time to time.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:30.490
So we thought it should be easy
to create, manage, schedule,

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:34.030
and monitor workflows across
Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.040
I call out all of those
steps individually,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.250
because they're all really
important parts when

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.600
you think about the
lifecycle of a workflow.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.472
It's not about just scheduling
something and letting it run.

00:02:43.472 --> 00:02:45.180
It's not about just
monitoring something.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.070
I mean, we really wanted to
think about the whole process

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.290
holistically together
and find something

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.130
that would allow somebody
to create this workflow,

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:57.190
to schedule it to run, to
look at how it's running,

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:00.800
and then manage that workflow
based on what's happening.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.850
So we came up with
Cloud Composer.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.167
Cloud Composer, if you
didn't catch last week,

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:07.000
because we didn't really
make a lot of noise

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.520
because Next was this
week, Cloud Composer

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.870
just recently went GA.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.690
So it's generally available
in a couple of regions

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:15.720
inside of Cloud Platform.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.740
And it's based on
Apache Airflow.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.410
So the best summary
of Cloud Composer

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.175
is it's managed Apache Airflow.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.770
So there's a few
things that we really

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.810
wanted to tackle with Composer.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.320
First, we wanted an end
to end GCP-wide solution

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.560
to orchestration and workflows.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.810
Second, it was really
important to us

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.490
that Composer works both inside
of Cloud Platform and outside.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.950
To be totally blunt, if
we developed something

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.260
that just worked
inside of Google Cloud,

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.910
it really would be
missing the mark.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:53.770
Because hybrid cloud,
multi-cloud, and just not

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:56.257
locking people in are
all a fact of life.

00:03:56.257 --> 00:03:58.340
So if we developed something
that was proprietary,

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.880
we thought, from the
outset, we would be failing.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.560
We wanted it to be really easy.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.987
We don't want a really
complicated workflow system.

00:04:05.987 --> 00:04:07.570
Because then people
are just wrangling

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.670
with the infrastructure,
the description language,

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:10.520
and that just sucks.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.754
It's not a great use of time.

00:04:12.754 --> 00:04:14.170
It was also really
important to us

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.640
that it's open source,
for a few reasons.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.370
Again, we don't want
you to be locked in.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.411
We want people to be able
to look under the hood,

00:04:20.411 --> 00:04:21.589
see what's happening.

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:25.060
We also wanted people to be
able to contribute and to be

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.050
part of a larger community.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.050
A common question we've
gotten since we launched

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.320
Airflow into alpha and
then beta and GAs, what's

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.860
the difference between Composer
as a managed Apache Airflow

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.670
service and just running
Airflow on my own

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.790
on VM or a set of VMs?

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:45.910
So there's a few things
that we tried to tackle.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.960
First, we wanted a seamless
and integrated experience

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.130
with Cloud Platform.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.830
That means that Composer
is available inside

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.120
of our command line tooling.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.160
There's a Google API.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.980
It's not just a
standalone thing that

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.960
feels like a separate product.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.970
Second, we wanted security.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.370
With Cloud Composer,
you have IAM.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:07.810
You have audit logging.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.610
It's not just a
separate product.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:12.550
It acts and fees, from a
security and auditability

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.840
perspective, the same as
any other Cloud product.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.610
We also wanted it to be easy
to use when things weren't

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.840
going quite the way you expect.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:27.360
So when you're developing with
workflows or running workflows,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.930
some pieces of that workflow may
not always run exactly the way

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.040
that you want.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.410
So Stackdriver integration
was very important to us.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.590
And it's a first class
citizen with Composer.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:40.920
We also wanted to make it really
easy to manage your Airflow

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:41.770
deployment.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.840
So that doesn't just mean
creating and deleting

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:45.840
your Airflow deployment.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.810
It means doing things like
setting environment variables,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.090
or installing and
maintaining Python packages.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.090
Things that you could
conceivably do on your own,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.630
but aren't a value add when
you're doing it on your own.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.290
Because we've built
on Apache Airflow,

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.660
there's a core set of support
for several products inside

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:04.970
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.890
So for example, there's
data flow operators.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:09.570
There's BigQuery operators.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.250
There's Cloud storage operators.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.670
And the support for Google
Cloud Platform products

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:17.010
is expanding with
each Airflow release.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.220
And that's a core part of what
the Airflow team is doing,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.140
either inside of the
Airflow team themselves,

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.880
or with other teams
inside of Google.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:27.390
It's expanding the breadth
and depth of support

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:31.510
for Cloud Platform
products inside of Airflow.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.690
Really importantly, Airflow
supports a whole host

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:37.190
of things outside of GCP.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.970
So it supports services.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.280
You can go talk to things
like Slack or JIRA.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.020
It supports technologies.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.880
You can go call REST APIs.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.230
You can go hit FTPs.

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:52.840
All of the support
for non-Google things

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:58.300
is absolutely usable
within Airflow and also

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.080
Cloud Composer.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.360
And we didn't want
to break or limit

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.920
what you can do outside
of GCP with Composer.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.650
So all of the cool
things that you

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:11.180
can do with Airflow outside
of GCP are very usable.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.820
Since our GA happened late
last week, not a lot of people

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.050
may have noticed.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.540
So we wanted to call out
a few things that just

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.300
launched last week with our GA.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:26.580
We launched support for a couple
of new regions inside of GCP.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.860
There's expanded
Stackdriver logging, which

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.280
you will see today in the demo.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.840
There is expanded
Cloud IAM rules.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.720
And we took a
bunch of fixes that

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.350
will appear in future versions
of Airflow or new additions,

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:44.310
and we backported them to
the Cloud Composer release.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:49.020
We try not, really, to modify
the Cloud Composer Airflow

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.170
version too much from the
mainline version of Airflow

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.820
itself, but once
in a while, there

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.910
are fixes, tweaks,
additions that we backport.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.100
Our general philosophy
is unless there's

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.790
a JIRA associated with it, we
won't inject it into Composer.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:07.390
Because again, we don't want
to make Composer a black box.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.540
It's just not a
value add, and it's

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.540
an alternative form of lock in.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.780
Since not a lot of people
may be familiar with Airflow

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.400
or may have just
heard of Airflow,

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.620
we wanted to quickly
cover Airflow,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.112
some of the core concepts,
just to establish a baseline.

00:08:23.112 --> 00:08:24.570
We're really excited
about Airflow.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:25.320
We love Airflow.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.900
And we want other people to
love Airflow and get excited

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.820
about Airflow.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.220
If you are totally
unfamiliar with Airflow,

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.990
it's an open source project.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.809
It's incubating in the
Apache Software Foundation.

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:38.789
It's been around
for a few years.

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:42.840
I think it's fair to say
it's become one of, if not

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.830
the leading open source
package to create and schedule

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.201
workflows.

00:08:48.201 --> 00:08:50.700
Airflow is really interesting,
because all of your workflows

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:51.420
are code.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.460
They're Python code.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:53.820
So they're highly approachable.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.864
You can do a lot of
different things.

00:08:55.864 --> 00:08:57.780
And you'll see part of
that in the demo today.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.780
You can do things like
programmatically generate

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.170
workflows, which is really cool.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.270
One of the questions we
faced, especially initially

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:08.660
when we made a bet on
Airflow about a year ago

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.602
was, why Airflow?

00:09:10.602 --> 00:09:12.310
What we really wanted
to do with Composer

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:15.690
is tie the strengths of Airflow
as an open source package

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.190
and open source community with
the strengths of Google Cloud

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:19.470
Platform.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.330
So Google Cloud
Platform, we were

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.070
very good at running
an infrastructure,

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.430
creating services, adding
layers of security,

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.380
auditability, maintaining costs.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:31.170
But as I mentioned,
we didn't have

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.540
a workflow and
orchestration solution.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.780
Airflow did have a really
strong workflow orchestration

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:37.770
solution.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.240
It had a whole
bunch of connectors

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.980
for services inside
of and outside

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.810
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.180
It already had a description
language, defined set of APIs.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:49.170
So we wanted to join
the two as a union

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.050
inside of Cloud Composer.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:52.830
As we've developed
in Cloud Composer,

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.750
we've also started contributing
back to the Airflow community.

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:58.219
The KubernetesExecutor
is a good example--

00:09:58.219 --> 00:10:00.510
and I'll talk about it a
little bit later in the deck--

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:03.150
of something that is really
interesting to us, something

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:05.730
that we're very interested in.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:07.440
There's a few key
concepts in Airflow

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.240
you may want to be
aware of if you've never

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.220
used Airflow in depth before.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.430
First, all your
workflows are graphs.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:17.130
So workflows are
a series of tasks

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.820
and the tasks can
fit into a graph.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:20.320
And that can be a
very simple graph.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.850
It can be just one node,
which is a bash script that

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.320
just says, tell the time.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.480
It can be a really
complicated graph,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:30.570
which we'll show you
an example of our

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.910
build system for Airflow,
which is a good example

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:34.350
of a complicated graph.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:36.140
And a graph has a
series of tasks.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:39.390
Tasks are essentially steps,
something that happens.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:40.830
Maybe you're
running a SQL query,

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.350
running a BigQuery query.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:47.840
Airflow, in those tasks,
has operators and sensors.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.090
And operator is essentially
something that tells something

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.120
to do something.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:55.380
So a good example would be
a BigQuery query operator

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.960
tells BigQuery to
go run a query.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.340
There's also the concept
of a sensor in Airflow,

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:03.840
which is essentially a binary
weight until something happens.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.240
When it's true, it precedes.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.340
Airflow itself also has a
lot of really interesting

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:14.880
deep functionality that
we evangelize people

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.770
use inside of Composer.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.200
It's also another
reason why we thought

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.300
that Airflow is a great bet.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.190
You can do things like
define connections

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:25.410
and have your workflows
use certain connections.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.350
You can set SLAs
on your workflows

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.480
and see what's
meeting SLA and not.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.911
You can do things like pass
information between tasks.

00:11:33.911 --> 00:11:35.910
There's a lot of really
interesting and advanced

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.460
functionality inside of Airflow.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.070
Generally, we get a lot of
questions of can Airflow

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.510
do x, y, or z.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.890
Often the answer is yes.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:46.694
Airflow actually
can do x, y, and z.

00:11:46.694 --> 00:11:48.360
And with that, I'm
going to turn it over

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.110
for a look at Airflow in depth.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:51.210
FENG LU: Thank you, James.

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:54.630
So with that being said,
we do want to-- next,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.930
we're going to review some
of the product details,

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.420
the way how we construct a
product and some of the design

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:04.770
decisions that we have made, so
you know the capabilities where

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:07.340
Composer is.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:08.930
So in Cloud Composer.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:12.680
we introduced this new
concept called environment.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:18.170
Really is very similar to a
Kubernetes cluster or data

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.060
cloud cluster.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:24.100
Essentially, it means a
collection of managed GCP

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:27.050
resources that gives you
the functionality needed

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:29.960
to run Apache Airflow.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.980
Inside a single GCB project, you
could create multiple composing

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:35.090
environments.

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:38.040
And all the environments
are integrated

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:41.870
was Google Cloud Storage,
[INAUDIBLE] stack travelogues,

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:46.470
as well as Cloud [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:51.390
So the way to interact with the
product, the following-- you

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:53.560
could use Cloud SDK.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:57.060
You could use [? Pantheon. ?]
You could use REST API.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.300
Functionality wise, all
three masters are equivalent.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:05.010
However, I do want to point
out one difference in Cloud SDK

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:08.460
to make it convenient
for the Composer users

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:12.750
and so that you don't have to
manage two set of command line

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:13.620
tools.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:15.300
There's Composer
command line tools.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.180
But at the same time, that's
also like Airflow command line

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:18.940
tools.

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.250
So what we did in the
product is we sort of,

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:26.115
like, a tunnel through Airflow
commands through the Composer

00:13:26.115 --> 00:13:27.240
[INAUDIBLE] Cloud commands.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:30.510
So that in a single place, you
can manage both your Composer

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:32.460
environment at the same
time you can interact

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.121
with the Airflow environment.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.890
Earlier I mentioned that a
composing environment is really

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:44.510
a collection of GCP resources.

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:47.030
So here, I'm going to give
you like a zoom one level

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:50.600
in, and then explain
how and why we decided

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.860
to use the following GCP
resources to construct

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.960
the Composer service.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:14:00.290
At a very high level, you
notice there are two projects.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:01.735
One is called a
customer project.

00:14:01.735 --> 00:14:03.610
The other one is called
a and tenant project.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:07.910
So customer project are probably
the ones you're familiar with.

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:10.880
Interact with GCP by
creating a GCP project.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:12.730
That's the customer project.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:16.750
And tenant project
is a concept new.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.960
Really, it's the same
as ordinary GCP project.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:22.120
It's just the case that
this tenant project

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:23.960
is managed and owned by Google.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.560
As we walk through the
detailed architecture,

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:32.170
we explain why we decided
to make this design decision

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:35.110
that a portion of the
resource actually lives

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:38.610
inside the tenant project.

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:42.340
Airflow itself, you know, if you
look at the way how it's been

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:45.820
constructed, it has a number
of microservice--like flavor.

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:47.840
You have an Airflow web server.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:51.670
You have a Airflow apps
scheduler, Airflow metadata

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:52.570
database.

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:55.720
Those components just
naturally match in a map

00:14:55.720 --> 00:15:00.730
to the wide range of
GCP services we offered.

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:03.220
So for example, I started
with Airflow scheduler

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:05.410
and Airflow worker.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.210
If you look inside
the Kubernetes class,

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:11.530
we decided to host both the
worker and the scheduler

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:13.280
inside Kubernetes cluster.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:16.930
The reason we do
that is so it allows

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:20.210
you to conveniently package
your workflow application

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:21.490
dependencies.

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:26.290
Because essentially,
all your tasks

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:29.330
can run inside the containers.

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:32.880
The worker and the schedule and
the communicator [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:35.350
through the [INAUDIBLE]
executor set up.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:39.250
And then moving next, we
have this Cloud SQL proxy.

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:42.050
And then, we decide to
host the Airflow metadata

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.740
database inside
a tenant project,

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:47.230
so that only the
service account you

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:49.240
to create a composing
environment has

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.790
access to an metadata database.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:53.810
It's really for
enhancing the security,

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.100
as we believe that a Cloud
SQL or the alpha database

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.050
house all the valuable metadata
information regarding workflow.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:03.010
Think about if you have
connection credentials stored

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:03.850
in a database.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:06.550
You obviously don't want
anyone in your project

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:09.910
be able to access that
credential information.

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.590
Walk down the right side.

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:14.320
We have the Airflow
web server interacts

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:19.340
with the database surface,
all the work flow information.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:23.500
And now, we decided to host
the web server inside GAE

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.660
so that it's partly accessible.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.060
You don't need to have
the clumsy proxy set up

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:29.950
to be able to access
the web server.

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:31.757
But we do realize
at the same time,

00:16:31.757 --> 00:16:33.340
you don't want to
make your web server

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:35.810
open to anyone on the internet.

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:38.850
So as a result, we collaborated
with a another service

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:41.110
in Google Cloud--

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.930
it's called
identity-aware proxy--

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:47.440
so that only authorized
users would be

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:49.590
able to access the web server.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.220
Later on in the time
of session, we'll

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:54.160
be able to give you a way to
have a sense and a feel of how

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.060
that works.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.620
We also make it extremely easy
for you to configure access

00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:00.550
to web server.

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:06.760
It's exactly the same way as how
you would configure IAM policy.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:09.930
Moving left, we have GCS.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.690
We use GCS as a
convenience store for you

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:14.290
to stage your decks.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:16.890
Deploying a new
workflow to Composer

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:20.640
is as simple as dropping
a file into a GCS bucket.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.230
We understand that
there's need, sometimes

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:26.700
you need to stage your
workflow artifacts.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:30.820
And we also make it a
managed service for you

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:34.680
so that your artifacts is nicely
staged in the GCS bucket, which

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.781
later allows you to sort of
retrieve those artifacts back

00:17:37.781 --> 00:17:38.280
out.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:44.820
Finally, everything's
being-- all the interactions,

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:49.680
all the logs are being
streamed to Stackdriver logs.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:51.060
Airflow has a very--

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:54.930
the UI itself comes
together with task logs.

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:57.480
But you can't really
find out what's

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:01.610
happening if, for example,
there's a Airflow worker crash,

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:03.980
or there's an Airflow
exception in itself,

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.690
the workflow scheduler
runs into exception.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.740
At that point, you sort
of lose the visibility

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:10.630
on what's happening.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.830
That's why we decided that
it makes a lot of sense

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:16.901
to offer this additional
Stackdriver logging capability.

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:23.200
With the architecture--
and now I'm

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:25.990
going to explain a little
bit about workflows.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:30.040
Because not everyone is sort
of familiar with workflow

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:34.180
and not the way how
Airflow express workflows.

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:36.730
So at a very high
level, a workflow

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.970
consists of a
collection of tasks

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:44.870
and their interdependency
relationships.

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:51.370
So in this case, for example,
you have some following HDFS.

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:54.190
And then, whenever you have
new [INAUDIBLE] addition

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:56.500
to HDFS, your
workflow-- you may want

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.750
to kick off your workflow
such that you will

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:02.000
copy the file from HDFS to GCS.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.530
Once it's in GCS, or
Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:09.190
maybe you want to trigger
a BigQuery operator that

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:12.430
does something, load the data
in, and then subsequently maybe

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.070
run some query, and make
the result available maybe

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.440
while a [INAUDIBLE]
notification.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:22.150
So a few things you probably
have noticed in this example

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:24.910
workflow description,
there are tasks

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:26.540
associated with workflows.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:30.150
For example, you want
to run a BigQuery job.

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:31.720
There are also dependencies.

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:34.900
You want to wait until the
data is available in GCS

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:37.269
and then you start
your BigQuery job.

00:19:37.269 --> 00:19:39.310
There's also a component
of triggering something.

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:41.143
You know, I have a file
that all of a sudden

00:19:41.143 --> 00:19:44.000
now appears in my on-prem HDFS.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.040
Then that disturbs my workflow.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:49.960
So those are some of the
elements or building blocks

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:52.900
in Airflow workflows.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.060
I'm going to start with
actually give everyone

00:19:55.060 --> 00:20:01.750
an example how you can build
a very simple workflow that

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:04.730
consists of two or three tasks.

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:09.100
So really, to Airflow,
unlike other sort

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.990
of orchestration
workflow solutions,

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:16.190
they express workflow as code.

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:19.480
So instead of having this
giant configuration file

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:22.090
expressing your workflows,
you write your workflow

00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:24.147
as Python code.

00:20:24.147 --> 00:20:25.980
Roughly speaking, there
are about five steps

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:29.260
for you to define a
workflow in Airflow.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:33.890
The first thing
is, like, import.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:35.870
Form the Airflow system.

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:39.150
You import the DAG,
which is the acronym

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:41.610
for direct [INAUDIBLE]
graph, which is really

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:43.950
another way of saying workflow.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:48.170
And then you try to
import all the operators

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:50.400
that you're going to
use in a workflow,

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.010
in this case, a
BigQuery operator.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:55.680
And then also
trigger rules, which

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:00.820
allows you to specify
[? intertask ?] relationship.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:02.790
Once you have all
import statements ready,

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:05.520
the next step is for you
to define all the arguments

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:08.830
to your task or
to your workflow.

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:11.520
You probably noticed that
there's a default DAG args.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:13.690
This is really a
very convenient thing

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:15.000
that's provided in Airflow.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:18.690
So that if you do feel that
you have a common arguments

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:22.020
to a number of
your tasks, instead

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.720
of specifying those arguments
at each and every single Airflow

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:29.144
task, you can specify them at
the beginning of your workflow,

00:21:29.144 --> 00:21:30.810
and then just pass
that in automatically

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:32.445
by the Airflow DAG model.

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:37.630
Imagine if you are going to
use a configuration, that's

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:38.340
probably harder.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:40.420
Because you probably
have to copy and paste

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:45.410
a lot of duplicated
lines of code.

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:48.700
Once you have all the
workflow data specified,

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:52.490
now you're going to
define your workflow.

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:56.790
There you give a name, you
give a schedule interval,

00:21:56.790 --> 00:22:00.950
are you passing a
default args assignation.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:04.670
Now within the DAG, you
start to define your tasks.

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:06.790
In this case, we have two tasks.

00:22:06.790 --> 00:22:09.020
You have BigQuery operator task.

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:12.110
You also have a BigQuery
to GCS operator.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:14.630
As James mentioned
earlier, Airflow

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:18.540
has a wide range of
supported GCP operators.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:22.980
Both BigQuery operator as well
as the BigQuery Cloud Storage

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:23.480
operator.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:24.920
They are all
available in Airflow.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:30.640
Once you specify all the
tasks and define all the task

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:32.620
arguments, the next
step is actually

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:36.670
for you to chain them up
by giving some dependency.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:39.700
In this specific
example, the line

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:44.420
simply says, hey, I want to run
bq_airflow_commits_query first.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:49.390
After that, I run the export
to GCS task, as simple as that.

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:58.360
Now the reason why we decided
to go with this route in Airflow

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:02.520
and Composer is really there
are a lot of nice things

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:05.320
that you can do once
DAG or workflow can

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:07.735
be specified as programs.

00:23:07.735 --> 00:23:10.980
It gives you vision control.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:13.910
It allows you to
dynamically generate DAGS.

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:17.650
It also give you the choice
of support templates,

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:19.750
so that you have a
[INAUDIBLE] of your workflow

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:25.250
and then be able to dynamically
instantiate your workflows.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:30.910
Like I said, the first thing
with DAG was, in Airflow DAGs,

00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:34.860
the language choice
supports Jinja templating.

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:38.390
So you can specify
a template command.

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:41.850
And every single run, you would
be able to reconfig that task.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.410
Likewise, think about
your need to generate

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:49.584
1,000 tasks, or 10,000
task of the same type.

00:23:49.584 --> 00:23:51.000
It's probably
fairly tedious to be

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.980
able to do that in a
configuration language.

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:57.870
So with DAG as code, it's just
like a simple two line of code

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:00.880
will give you 1,000 tasks.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:05.150
The third thing is Airflow
sort of naturally match

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:10.020
the programming or the software
model or software development

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:13.230
process, where you have
modules and submodules.

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:18.620
So here in Airflow, you have
workflows and sub workflows.

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.952
They're called DAGs and subDAGs.

00:24:20.952 --> 00:24:22.410
So in the example
on the left side,

00:24:22.410 --> 00:24:24.150
you could have many
tasks, or if you

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:27.420
want to realize that you want
to create a reusable DAGs, what

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:30.900
you can do is just package
those DAGs into a subDAG

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:35.620
and include a subDAG,
in the [INAUDIBLE] DAG.

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:38.770
So with all that, I'm going
to give a composite demo.

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:42.390
So in that demo, we're
going to show you

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:44.950
how to interact with a product.

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:48.140
And also, for example, we just
cover how does that look like?

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.230
How do you query
a workflow status?

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:53.500
How would you trigger
workflow execution

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:54.630
from a external system?

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:01.797
All right.

00:25:01.797 --> 00:25:03.380
Now we're going to
switch to the demo.

