OK.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:09.140
There we go.

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:10.060
Cool.

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:11.277
FENG LU: So we have--

00:26:11.277 --> 00:26:11.860
let's go back.

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:15.940
We have a very simple
and a niche Cloud Console

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:18.460
interface for you to interact
with the environment.

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:20.640
So to create a
composing environment,

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:24.970
that's where you need
to do, specify name--

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:26.780
Google Next.

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:28.400
Specify a number
of nodes you want,

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.620
like three nodes or
10 nodes, how many

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:32.060
number nodes that you want.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:34.120
Which location you
want to deploy--

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:37.110
that's an [INAUDIBLE] one.

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:39.720
You have the option to
define a machine type.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:41.550
If you do feel that
you're going to have

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:43.800
some CPU-intensive
workflows, you

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:47.041
can try to config your composing
environment with some more

00:26:47.041 --> 00:26:47.790
powerful machines.

00:26:51.010 --> 00:26:55.260
And likewise, you can also
specify network and subnet.

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:59.100
This is for the case when you
need to have a shared VPC,

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.210
or even like reuse
some network we

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:03.870
have defined in your project.

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:06.470
There's one thing
I want to call out

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:09.060
in the configuration is that
we do allow you to provide

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:10.839
your own service account.

00:27:10.839 --> 00:27:13.380
You don't have to rely on the
default computer engine service

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:13.880
account.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:18.570
Instead, you can supply any
service account you gave.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:22.020
This allows you to
sort of restrict

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:26.010
the possible set of services
that your Composer environment

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:29.510
can possibly interact with.

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:33.510
We do allow you to have Airflow
configuration overrides.

00:27:33.510 --> 00:27:36.440
For example, if you want
to increase your DAG load

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:39.630
timeout value from 100
seconds to 200 seconds.

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:42.600
This way you can specify some
of the Airflow configuration

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:44.900
overrides.

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:48.614
Once you have all the parameters
input, just create [INAUDIBLE]..

00:27:51.771 --> 00:27:52.270
Yeah.

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:54.190
That's what you need
to do to bring up

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:57.760
a composing environment.

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:00.040
While waiting for the
environment to be created,

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:01.360
right now it takes a while.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:05.890
Because as I mentioned, we
host the Airflow web server

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:10.270
inside GAE, and it simply
takes GAE 10-plus minutes

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:13.090
to get the application
deployed for you.

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:18.294
So we have pre-created a
Composer demo environment.

00:28:18.294 --> 00:28:19.210
So we can take a look.

