00:34:58.190 --> 00:35:02.170
This is what I mentioned, that
Airflow has this directory scan

00:35:02.170 --> 00:35:05.300
interval, which allows you
to specify how often you want

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:08.250
to scan for new changes,
new files, or new workflows

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:09.960
in your DAG folder.

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:11.960
Again, there's
some default value.

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:13.880
But through
Composer, we gave you

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:16.550
the option to override that
default configuration value.

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:25.180
While waiting for the
workflow demo to show up,

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:28.240
we can look even more into
details about what this Airflow

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:30.820
UI offers to you.

00:35:30.820 --> 00:35:32.800
You have different
views of your DAG.

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:36.220
It is the graph view, true view.

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:38.530
You can also see the
code at any time,

00:35:38.530 --> 00:35:40.630
just in case you need
to sort of switch

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:43.990
back and forth between the graph
representation of your workflow

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:47.620
and the code representation.

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:53.740
You could also conveniently add
connections to your workflows.

00:35:53.740 --> 00:35:58.660
Only imagine that Airflow
does store all the connection

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:01.090
credentials in the
metadata database.

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:04.420
And they also conveniently offer
you this Airflow, this web UI,

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:07.420
for you to mutate
your connections.

00:36:07.420 --> 00:36:11.080
Any time you can also see all
your configuration that comes

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.630
together was this
particular installation.

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:15.550
As I mentioned,
those configuration

00:36:15.550 --> 00:36:19.270
can be later on changed
through the manager service.

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:23.880
And let's come back now.

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:29.300
We do notice that, as we added
this Kubernetes pod example,

00:36:29.300 --> 00:36:32.280
it's pretty straightforward.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:33.950
Let's take a look at the code.

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:41.010
So as I mentioned, you
have all the-- just

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:44.290
to recap-- you have all the
import statements at the top.

00:36:44.290 --> 00:36:47.400
And then you have all your
definition, all your workflow

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.100
data that you're going to
[INAUDIBLE] in your workflow.

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:51.950
Now you define your DAG.

00:36:51.950 --> 00:36:53.980
After that, you
define your task.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:55.542
So in this case,
we have two tasks.

00:36:55.542 --> 00:36:57.000
The task in the
first one is really

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.941
like a batch
operator [INAUDIBLE]..

00:36:59.941 --> 00:37:01.440
The second one is
what I mentioned--

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:05.240
you have a Kubernetes pod
operator, which allows you to,

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:08.520
in this case, get a
per image and a compute

00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:09.390
of the value of pi.

00:37:16.570 --> 00:37:18.550
At any time, you could--

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:28.270
this is the Airflow web UI where
you can inspect the log output.

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:33.240
Sometimes there might be a delay
in how soon those logs appear.

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:38.410
This is where the Stackdriver
logging is helpful.

00:37:38.410 --> 00:37:41.650
So let's try to look at the logs
that's viewed at the same time

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:42.580
by the worker.

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:08.850
As you notice, we have
this pod launcher.

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:11.730
That's actually related
to launching the pod.

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:17.430
So the nice thing we added
into the Stackdriver log

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:20.670
is that we organized
the Stackdriver log,

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:23.890
and then provide labels
for you to access the task.

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:24.599
So I'm going to--

00:38:24.599 --> 00:38:25.765
here's what I'm going to do.

00:38:25.765 --> 00:38:27.600
I'm going to field out
all logs pertaining

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:28.640
to this specific task.

00:38:37.619 --> 00:38:38.160
There you go.

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:41.740
You see all the logs that
are being generated for you.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:44.790
While, at the same time, it
